export const HP1={title:'The event and what judges reward',hook:'A single photograph can show what a career really looks like—the focus in a phlebotomist’s hands, the reassurance in a nurse’s face, the precision of a pharmacist at work. Health Career Photography asks you to capture three different health professionals in action, write about each career and present your photos to judges. It is part career exploration, part photography and part storytelling.',objectives:['Describe the event’s rounds, display time and scoring.','Explain the photo subject requirements.','Identify the portfolio contents and order.','Plan a timeline from contacting professionals to presenting.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Health Career Photography is a **solo event** in the **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** divisions. You photograph **three different health professionals** in **three different fields** performing an aspect of their jobs, edit the photos as needed, write a **one-page narrative** for each career and present the three printed photos to judges.',
'**Round One** is the pre-judged **portfolio**, uploaded as **one combined PDF** by **May 15**, worth **165 points**. At ILC, you attend the **Required Project Display Time** with your physical portfolio (missing it costs **15 points**). Finalists give a presentation of up to **five minutes**—about one and a half minutes per photo—worth **110 points**.',
'New for 2026–27, the presentation is a maximum of five minutes and pre-judging and advancement were clarified.',
"Because this is a solo event, you handle every part yourself: contacting professionals, photographing, editing, writing and presenting. Strong organization and an early start matter even more than in team events.",
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Part','What you do','Points'],rows:[['Round One','Pre-judged portfolio PDF (3 photos, narratives, permissions)','165'],['Display Time','Stand with your portfolio at ILC','−15 if missed'],['Round Two','Up to 5-minute presentation of the three photos','110'],['Total','Round One + Round Two','275']]}]],
['The photo subject rules',[
'The main subject of each photo must be a **health professional in action**—visible, identifiable and doing something that shows the profession or the professional’s skills. An observer should be able to tell **which health profession** is shown just by looking.',
'Photos of only medical **procedures** or **body parts** without a career reference do **not** meet requirements, and photos **including the competitor** are not allowed. All photos must be **HIPAA-compliant**. The three professionals must be in **three different fields**—for example, nurse, pharmacist and physical therapist, not dentist, dental hygienist and dental assistant.',
"If you are unsure whether a career counts as a health career, check the NCHSE and Explore Health Careers lists before scheduling. Discovering a problem after the shoot can mean starting over.",
sort('Meets the photo rules?',['Meets the rules','Does not meet the rules'],[['A respiratory therapist adjusting a ventilator',0],['A close-up of a wound with no professional visible',1],['A pharmacist counseling a consenting adult about a prescription',0],['The competitor posing in scrubs',1],['A physical therapist guiding a patient through an exercise (with permission)',0],['A dentist, dental hygienist and dental assistant as the three subjects',1]])]],
['The portfolio',[
'The physical portfolio uses an **official HOSA portfolio or notebook from the HOSA Store** (worth 5 points in Round Two). In order: **Page 1** title page; **Page 2** Photo #1 (8″ × 10″, in a clear sheet protector, numbered in the bottom right corner); **Page 3** Photo #1 narrative; **Pages 4–5** Photo #1 facility and subject permission forms; then Photo #2 (pages 6–9) and Photo #3 (pages 10–13) in the same pattern.',
'The title page lists the event name, your name, HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association, and all three career titles, centered and neat.',
"Keep the portfolio pages clean and consistent. Judges see the PDF first, then the physical portfolio at ILC, so neatness in both forms supports a professional impression.",
order('Portfolio order',['Title page','Photo #1 (8″ × 10″, numbered)','Photo #1 narrative','Photo #1 facility and subject permission forms','Photo #2, narrative and permission forms','Photo #3, narrative and permission forms'])]],
['How judges score',[
'**Round One (165):** overall portfolio—title page, photos numbered and narrative formatting (5 each) and three different professionals in three different fields (15); then, for **each** photo, **focus of occupation**, **originality**, **narrative description** and **proper exposure and framing** (10 each) and **permission forms** (5).',
'**Round Two (110):** for **each** photo, **understanding of the profession**, **description of the photo** and **connection to the health care system** (10 each); **delivery**—voice, stage presence and diction (5 each); and **official HOSA notebook or portfolio used** (5).',
'Each photo carries 45 points in Round One and 30 in Round Two, so all three matter equally.',
"Notice that permission forms are scored for every photo. A beautiful photo without both forms loses points, so treat paperwork as part of the photography.",
match('Rubric item and value',[['Three different fields','15 (Round One)'],['Focus of occupation (per photo)','10'],['Permission forms (per photo)','5'],['Connection to health care system (per photo)','10 (Round Two)'],['Official HOSA portfolio','5 (Round Two)'],['Narrative formatting','5']])]],
['A project timeline',[
'**Early fall:** choose three careers in different fields and contact professionals and facilities. **Fall and winter:** schedule shoots, get permission forms signed, photograph and interview each professional. **Winter:** edit photos, print 8″ × 10″ copies and write narratives. **Early spring:** assemble the portfolio, scan it into one PDF and upload well before **May 15**. **Spring:** rehearse the presentation.',
'Plan extra time: facilities may need weeks to approve photography, and you may need to reshoot.',
"Keep a simple project log with each contact, date, location and status. When several facilities are involved, a log keeps follow-ups and permission forms from slipping through the cracks.",
order('Photography project timeline',['Choose three careers in different fields','Contact professionals and facilities','Get permission forms signed','Photograph and interview each professional','Edit, print and write narratives','Assemble, scan and upload by May 15','Rehearse the presentation'])]],
['Learning from the experience',[
'Beyond photography, this event is career exploration. Each shoot is a chance to **ask questions** about responsibilities, education, benefits and challenges—information you need for the narrative and the presentation. Take notes during or right after each visit.',
'Judges look for evidence that **you took the photos** and understood what was happening; your notes and memories of each shoot help you describe the photo vividly in Round Two.',
"Reflect after each shoot on what you learned about the career. These reflections become the most personal and memorable parts of your narratives and presentation.",
chk('Why take notes during each photo shoot?',['They provide details for the narrative and presentation','They are required by HIPAA','They replace permission forms','They are not useful'],'Details bring the photo to life.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Planning the shoots',{a:{text:'You plan to photograph a registered nurse, a licensed practical nurse and a nursing assistant. (Scenario invented for practice.) Does this meet the rules?',choices:[['No—choose three different fields, such as nursing, pharmacy and physical therapy','b','Correct.',true],['Yes—they are different jobs','b','They are all in nursing; the rubric wants different fields.',false]]},b:{text:'A friend offers to photograph you assisting a nurse. Can you use that photo?',choices:[['No—photos including the competitor do not meet requirements','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, if you are wearing scrubs','c','The competitor must not appear.',false]]},c:{text:'You select a nurse, a pharmacist and a physical therapist.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Solo event (secondary and postsecondary): photograph three health professionals in three different fields in action; Round One portfolio PDF (165 points, by May 15); required display time (−15 if missed); Round Two presentation up to 5 minutes (110 points).','Each photo must show an identifiable professional performing their job; no photos of only procedures or body parts, no photos of the competitor and all photos HIPAA-compliant.','The official HOSA portfolio holds a title page and, for each photo, the 8″ × 10″ numbered photo, a one-page narrative and facility and subject permission forms; each photo carries 45 Round One and 30 Round Two points.'],terms:[['Health professional in action','A professional visibly performing an aspect of their job.'],['Permission form','A signed form allowing a facility or subject to be photographed.'],['Portfolio','The official HOSA notebook holding photos, narratives and forms.']],mcqs:[q('How many health professionals must be photographed?','Three, in three different fields','Two','Five','One','Rule.'),q('What size must each printed photo be?','8″ × 10″','4″ × 6″','11″ × 14″','5″ × 7″','Rule.'),q('Which photo does NOT meet requirements?','A photo including the competitor','A pharmacist counseling','A therapist guiding exercise','A nurse checking vitals','Rule.'),q('How many points is Round One worth?','165','110','275','100','Pre-judged.'),q('What is the maximum presentation time?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','New for 2026–27.'),q('Which set shows three different fields?','Nurse, pharmacist, physical therapist','Dentist, dental hygienist, dental assistant','RN, LPN, CNA','Three surgeons','Rule.'),q('What is the penalty for missing display time?','15 points from Round Two','5 points','Disqualification','None','Rule.'),q('Which portfolio earns points in Round Two?','An official HOSA notebook or portfolio','Any folder','A plastic bag','A digital-only copy','Rubric.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Health Career Photography event, its photo requirements and how you would plan your project.',rubric:[['Event',['solo','three','165','110','display time','5 minutes','may 15']],['Photo rules',['professional','action','identifiable','different fields','competitor','procedures','hipaa']],['Portfolio',['title page','8″ × 10″','narrative','permission','official','order']],['Planning',['contact','facility','permission','interview','edit','upload','rehearse']]],model:'Health Career Photography is a solo event for secondary and postsecondary members in which I photograph three different health professionals in three different fields, such as a registered nurse, a pharmacist and a physical therapist, each performing an aspect of their job. Each professional must be visible and identifiable, and a viewer should be able to tell the profession from the photo; photos of only procedures or body parts, photos that include me and photos that violate HIPAA do not meet the rules. In Round One, judges score my portfolio, uploaded as one combined PDF by May 15, for 165 points. The physical portfolio, in an official HOSA notebook, contains a title page with my information and the three career titles, then for each photo an 8-by-10-inch print in a sheet protector numbered in the bottom right corner, a one-page narrative about the career and the facility and subject permission forms. At ILC, I must attend the Required Project Display Time or lose 15 points, and if I advance, I present the three photos in up to five minutes for 110 points. My plan would be to choose three careers in early fall, contact professionals and facilities, get permission forms signed, schedule shoots and interview each professional while photographing, then edit and print the photos and write the narratives in winter, assemble and scan the portfolio for an early upload and rehearse my presentation in spring.'},sources:[RULES,NCHSE]};

export const HP2={title:'Choosing and contacting three professionals',hook:'Your three subjects shape everything: the photos you can take, the stories you can tell and the careers you will learn about. The rubric awards 15 points for clearly showing three different health professionals in three different fields. Choosing wisely—and contacting professionals professionally—sets up a successful project.',objectives:['Choose three careers in three different fields.','Use career resources to confirm health careers.','Contact professionals and facilities professionally.','Prepare questions for each shoot.'],sections:[
['Choosing three different fields',[
'Choose careers that are clearly **different**, ideally from different pathways: **therapeutic services** (nurses, physical therapists, respiratory therapists, pharmacists), **diagnostic services** (radiologic technologists, sonographers, medical laboratory scientists, phlebotomists), **health informatics** (health information technicians), **support services** (biomedical equipment technicians, sterile processing technicians) and **biotechnology research and development** (research scientists).',
'Use the **NCHSE** and **Explore Health Careers** lists to confirm each career; the guidelines note that the NCHSE list has been updated to match **Advance CTE** career clusters, so check carefully.',
'Variety also makes your presentation more interesting.',
"Think about how the three careers will look together as a set. Variety in settings—a clinic, a pharmacy and a laboratory—also creates visual variety across your portfolio.",
sort('Three different fields?',['Good set','Too similar'],[['Nurse, pharmacist, physical therapist',0],['Dentist, dental hygienist, dental assistant',1],['Sonographer, respiratory therapist, medical lab scientist',0],['Three types of physicians in one clinic',1],['Phlebotomist, occupational therapist, paramedic',0]])]],
['Choosing photogenic careers',[
'Some careers are easier to photograph in action than others. Look for careers with **visible tasks**: a phlebotomist preparing a venipuncture, a sonographer using an ultrasound probe on a model, a physical therapist guiding exercise, a pharmacist measuring medication, a lab scientist using a microscope or pipette.',
'Consider **settings** you can access, such as school-based clinics, local pharmacies, therapy clinics, veterinary practices (if the career is classified as a health career), fire and EMS stations or hospital education departments. Training labs with mannequins can avoid patient privacy issues.',
'Choose careers you are curious about—your enthusiasm will show in Round Two.',
"Ask the professional which part of their job best represents the career. They often suggest a task that is both meaningful and photogenic, and it shows respect for their expertise.",
match('Career and photogenic task',[['Phlebotomist','Labeling blood tubes'],['Sonographer','Positioning an ultrasound probe on a volunteer or model'],['Physical therapist','Guiding a balance exercise'],['Pharmacist','Counting tablets at the counter'],['Medical laboratory scientist','Loading samples into an analyzer'],['Respiratory therapist','Setting up a nebulizer']])]],
['Contacting professionals',[
'Ask your advisor, family, school nurse or health science teacher for contacts. Many hospitals and clinics have **education** or **public relations** departments that handle photography requests. Send a short, professional email or call explaining who you are, the HOSA event, what you want to photograph, how long it will take and that you will bring permission forms.',
'Be flexible with scheduling, follow up politely if you do not hear back and always thank people for their time.',
"Start contacting professionals early in the fall. Approvals can take weeks, and some facilities need administrator sign-off before you can schedule a visit.",
ex('A professional request','Draft a short request email (details invented for practice).',['Greeting: “Dear Ms. Patel,”','Introduction: “I am a junior at Lincoln High School and a HOSA member.”','Purpose: “I am competing in Health Career Photography and would like to photograph a physical therapist at work for about 30 minutes.”','Details: “I will bring facility and subject permission forms and follow all privacy rules.”','Close: “Thank you for considering my request. Sincerely, Jordan Lee”'],'A clear, polite request with all key details (details invented for practice).')]],
['Facility requirements',[
'Facilities may have **photography policies**, require approval from administrators and limit where and when you can photograph. Ask about rules for patient areas, dress, infection control (hand hygiene, masks) and identification badges.',
'You need a **facility permission form** and a **subject permission form** for each photo. If you photograph two professionals at the same facility, include copies behind each photo’s narrative.',
'Respect every rule—you are representing HOSA and your school.',
"Bring a printed copy of the event guidelines in case a facility representative wants to understand exactly how the photos will be used.",
"Clarify with each facility whether staff badges or names may appear in photos. Some organizations prefer that badges be turned or blurred by framing, which you should plan for before shooting.",
chk('Two of your photos are taken at the same hospital. What do you include?',['Copies of the facility permission form behind each photo’s narrative','One form only for the whole portfolio','No facility form','A photo of the hospital sign instead'],'Each photo needs its forms.')]],
['Preparing interview questions',[
'Each narrative needs the professional’s **job responsibilities**, **required education and training**, **benefits and challenges** and other interesting information. Prepare questions: What does a typical day involve? What education and credentials did you need? What do you enjoy most? What is most challenging? How do you work with other team members? What advice would you give students?',
'These answers also help you explain **how the career fits into the health care system** in Round Two.',
"Record answers in your own words immediately after the interview while details are fresh. Accurate notes prevent errors in your narrative and help you answer judges confidently.",
flip('Interview questions',[['Typical day','What tasks do you perform most?'],['Education','What degree and credentials did you need?'],['Benefits','What do you enjoy most?'],['Challenges','What is hardest about your job?'],['Teamwork','Whom do you work with most?'],['Advice','What should students know?']])]],
['Professional conduct on site',[
'Arrive early, dress professionally (business casual or as the facility requests), wear any visitor badge and follow staff instructions. Keep your phone silent except for photography, stay out of the way of patient care and ask before moving equipment or entering rooms.',
'Afterward, send a **thank-you note**. Professionals who feel respected may share more information and even help again in the future.',
"Have a backup professional in mind for each career. If a shoot is canceled, a second contact keeps the project on schedule.",
sort('Professional behavior?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['Arriving early with signed forms ready',0],['Wandering into patient rooms without asking',1],['Following hand hygiene rules',0],['Posting photos on social media before approval',1],['Sending a thank-you note',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Making arrangements',{a:{text:'A clinic says photography is allowed only in the staff training room with a mannequin. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Accept and plan a photo of the professional demonstrating a skill on the mannequin','b','Correct.',true],['Sneak photos in patient areas','b','Follow facility rules.',false]]},b:{text:'A pharmacist agrees but asks that no customers appear in photos. How do you respond?',choices:[['Agree and frame the shot to show only the pharmacist and the work area','c','Correct.',true],['Include customers anyway','c','Respect privacy requests.',false]]},c:{text:'All three shoots are scheduled with permission.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Choose three careers from clearly different fields or pathways, confirmed with NCHSE (updated to Advance CTE clusters) and Explore Health Careers; three different fields earns 15 points.','Pick careers with visible, photogenic tasks and accessible settings, including training labs with mannequins; contact professionals and facilities politely with clear details and follow facility photography policies.','Each photo needs facility and subject permission forms (copies if the same facility); prepare questions on responsibilities, education, benefits, challenges and teamwork; behave professionally and send thank-you notes.'],terms:[['Career pathway','A group of related careers, such as diagnostic services.'],['Facility permission form','Authorization from the organization where a photo is taken.'],['Subject permission form','Authorization from the person photographed.']],mcqs:[q('Which set best meets the three-fields requirement?','Nurse, pharmacist, physical therapist','Dentist, hygienist, dental assistant','RN, LPN, CNA','Three surgeons','Rule.'),q('Which resource lists health careers?','NCHSE','A sports website','A movie database','A fashion blog','Resource.'),q('Which department often handles photography requests at hospitals?','Education or public relations','Cafeteria','Parking','Laundry','Contact.'),q('What should a request email include?','Who you are, the purpose, time needed and permission forms','Only your name','A demand for access','Nothing','Professionalism.'),q('Two photos at one facility require:','Copies of the facility form behind each photo','One form total','No forms','Only verbal permission','Rule.'),q('Which question helps the narrative?','What education did you need?','What is your salary history?','Where do you live?','How old are you?','Narrative.'),q('Which is professional on site?','Following hand hygiene rules','Entering rooms without asking','Posting photos immediately','Moving equipment freely','Conduct.'),q('Why choose careers with visible tasks?','They are easier to photograph in action','They pay more','They are required by HIPAA','They need no permission','Photogenic.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would choose three professionals and arrange photo shoots for Health Career Photography.',rubric:[['Choosing careers',['three','different fields','pathways','nchse','explore health careers','visible tasks']],['Contacting',['advisor','email','purpose','time','permission forms','follow up']],['Facility rules',['policy','facility permission','subject permission','copies','infection control','privacy']],['Preparation and conduct',['questions','responsibilities','education','benefits','challenges','professional','thank-you']]],model:'I would choose three careers from clearly different pathways—a physical therapist from therapeutic services, a sonographer from diagnostic services and a pharmacist from therapeutic services in a different field—confirming each on the NCHSE list, which now matches Advance CTE career clusters, and on Explore Health Careers. I would choose careers with visible tasks, such as a physical therapist guiding a balance exercise, a sonographer positioning a probe on a volunteer and a pharmacist preparing a prescription. I would ask my advisor and health science teacher for contacts and send each professional a short, polite email explaining that I am a HOSA member competing in Health Career Photography, what I would like to photograph, that it would take about 30 minutes and that I would bring facility and subject permission forms. For hospitals, I would contact the education or public relations department and ask about photography policies, dress, hand hygiene and badges. Each photo would need a signed facility permission form and subject permission form, with copies if two photos come from the same facility. Before each shoot, I would prepare questions about the professional’s typical day, education and credentials, benefits, challenges and teamwork for my narrative. On site, I would arrive early in business casual clothing, follow every staff instruction and privacy request, stay out of the way of patient care and send a thank-you note afterward.'},sources:[RULES,EHC]};

export const HP3={title:'Permissions, privacy and photo ethics',hook:'Health care settings are full of private moments. Every photo in this event must be HIPAA-compliant and backed by signed facility and subject permission forms—5 points per photo, and a requirement for an ethical project. Knowing how to protect privacy while still capturing compelling images is a professional skill you will use throughout a health career.',objectives:['Explain HIPAA basics relevant to photography.','Obtain and organize facility and subject permission forms.','Compose photos that protect patient privacy.','Apply ethical standards to health care photography.'],sections:[
['HIPAA and photography',[
'The **HIPAA Privacy Rule** protects individuals’ **protected health information (PHI)**—information that identifies a person and relates to their health, care or payment. Full-face photographs and comparable images are among the identifiers HIPAA lists. Health care facilities must protect patients’ privacy, so they control photography in their spaces.',
'For this event, all photos must be **HIPAA-compliant**. The safest approach is to photograph professionals performing tasks **without identifiable patients**—using mannequins, simulation labs, consenting volunteers or careful framing—and never to capture charts, screens, wristbands or names.',
'When in doubt, leave it out of the frame.',
"Even in non-hospital settings such as pharmacies or therapy clinics, privacy rules still apply. Customers, patients and their information should never be identifiable in your photos.",
sort('HIPAA risk in the photo?',['Risk','No risk'],[['A patient’s name visible on a monitor',0],['A nurse demonstrating on a mannequin',1],['A readable patient wristband',0],['A therapist with a consenting volunteer, no health details shown',1],['A whiteboard listing room numbers and diagnoses',0],['A pharmacist with only medication bottles visible (no labels readable)',1]])]],
['Permission forms',[
'The guidelines include two required forms for **each photo**: a **facility permission form** signed by an authorized facility representative and a **patient/subject permission form** signed by the person photographed (and anyone else identifiable). Place them behind each photo’s narrative; they should **not** go in clear sheet protectors.',
'If photos are taken in the same facility, place **copies** of the facility form behind each narrative. Missing forms cost 5 points per photo and can make a photo ineligible.',
"Read the permission forms carefully before the shoot so you can explain them clearly and fill in details such as the photo number and date accurately.",
"Keep a short checklist on your phone for each shoot: facility form signed, subject form signed, extra blanks packed and photo number recorded. Checking it before leaving prevents missing paperwork.",
order('Permission process for each photo',['Ask the facility about photography policy','Get the facility permission form signed','Explain the purpose to the subject','Get the subject permission form signed','Photograph within agreed limits','File forms behind the photo’s narrative'])]],
['Composing for privacy',[
'You can capture powerful images without revealing private information. Strategies include photographing from **behind or beside** a patient so their face is not visible, using **shallow focus** so backgrounds blur, framing **close on the professional’s hands and face**, using **mannequins or simulation** and shooting in **training labs**.',
'Check every frame for **screens, charts, labels, wristbands and name badges** of patients. Remove or turn away items before shooting, since adding graphics or blurring with added elements is not allowed in editing (cropping is allowed).',
"Before pressing the shutter, scan the entire frame from corner to corner. Small details at the edges—like a printed schedule or computer screen—are easy to miss.",
match('Privacy strategy and example',[['Simulation','Nurse practicing CPR on a mannequin'],['Angle','Shoot over the professional’s shoulder, patient not identifiable'],['Shallow focus','Background charts unreadable'],['Clear the frame','Turn monitor away before shooting'],['Crop','Remove an identifiable visitor at the edge'],['Volunteer','A consenting classmate acting as the patient']])]],
['Ethics beyond the rules',[
'Ethical photography means **respecting dignity**: never photograph people in vulnerable situations without clear consent, avoid images that embarrass or exploit and stop if anyone seems uncomfortable. Represent professionals **accurately**—do not stage misleading scenes or alter photos to change what happened.',
'Respect **copyright** too: your photos must be your own work, and you should not use others’ images. Do not share photos online without the facility’s and subject’s permission.',
"Represent the professional in a way they would be proud of. Show competence and care, and avoid unflattering or embarrassing moments even if they seem dramatic.",
"Remember that consent can be withdrawn. If a professional later decides they do not want a photo used, respect that decision and choose another image.",
chk('A professional asks you to delete one photo. What do you do?',['Delete it and thank them','Keep it secretly','Post it anyway','Argue until they agree'],'Respect consent.')]],
['Working with volunteers and minors',[
'If someone acts as a patient—a classmate or family member—get their signed subject permission form. For **minors**, follow school and facility policies, which often require a **parent or guardian’s signature**. Explain how the photo will be used: in your portfolio, the PDF upload, display time and presentation.',
'Volunteers should never undergo real procedures for a photo; simulate safely.',
"Store photos securely and delete images you will not use, especially any that accidentally include identifiable patients or private information.",
"If a facility asks to review your photos before you use them, allow time for that step and follow any requests to remove images.",
ex('Explaining photo use to a subject','Write what you would tell a subject before signing (details invented for practice).',['“These photos are for my HOSA Health Career Photography competition.”','“They will appear in my printed portfolio and an online upload judged by HOSA.”','“I will not include your name or any health information.”','“You may say no or ask me to delete a photo.”'],'A clear explanation supports informed permission (details invented for practice).')]],
['Keeping forms organized',[
'Bring **extra blank forms** to every shoot, fill in details (photo number, date, facility) neatly and check signatures before leaving. Keep forms in a folder and scan them for the PDF upload. Make copies for your records.',
'The subject and facility names on the forms should match your narrative and title page.',
"Keep copies of every signed form in a separate folder or scan. If the physical portfolio is damaged, you can reprint the forms quickly.",
sort('Good form management?',['Good','Poor'],[['Bringing extra blank forms to the shoot',0],['Forgetting to get the subject’s signature',1],['Checking every signature before leaving',0],['Putting forms inside the photo’s sheet protector',1],['Scanning forms clearly for the PDF',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Privacy decisions',{a:{text:'Your best shot of a nurse shows a patient’s face and a readable whiteboard. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Do not use it; reshoot with a mannequin or a framing that protects privacy','b','Correct.',true],['Add a blur graphic over the face','b','Adding graphics is not allowed; privacy must be protected in the shot.',false]]},b:{text:'You photograph two professionals at the same clinic. How do you handle facility forms?',choices:[['Place a copy of the facility form behind each photo’s narrative','c','Correct.',true],['Include only one form','c','Each photo needs its forms.',false]]},c:{text:'Your photos are compliant and fully documented.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['HIPAA protects identifiable health information, including full-face photos; all photos must be HIPAA-compliant—avoid identifiable patients, charts, screens, wristbands and names.','Each photo needs a facility permission form and a subject permission form placed behind its narrative (not in sheet protectors), with facility copies when photos share a location; missing forms cost 5 points per photo.','Protect privacy with simulation, angles, shallow focus and clearing the frame (cropping allowed, added graphics not); respect dignity, consent and copyright; get guardian signatures for minors and manage forms carefully.'],terms:[['HIPAA','A federal law protecting the privacy of health information.'],['Protected health information','Health information that can identify a person.'],['Simulation','Practicing or demonstrating care on mannequins or models.']],mcqs:[q('Which law protects patient health information?','HIPAA','FERPA only','OSHA','ADA only','Privacy.'),q('How many permission forms does each photo need?','Two: facility and subject','One','None','Three','Rule.'),q('Where do permission forms go?','Behind each photo’s narrative, not in sheet protectors','Inside the photo’s sheet protector','On the title page','Nowhere','Order.'),q('Which photo creates a HIPAA risk?','One showing a readable patient wristband','A nurse with a mannequin','A pharmacist with unlabeled bottles','A therapist with a consenting volunteer','Privacy.'),q('Is adding a blur graphic allowed in editing?','No—adding graphics is not permitted','Yes, always','Only on faces','Only in color','Editing rule.'),q('Which privacy strategy uses mannequins?','Simulation','Shallow focus','Cropping','Backlighting','Strategy.'),q('Minor volunteers often need:','A parent or guardian’s signature','No permission','Only verbal approval','A HOSA ID','Policy.'),q('If a subject asks you to delete a photo, you should:','Delete it','Keep it','Post it','Ignore them','Consent.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would protect privacy and obtain permissions for your health career photos.',rubric:[['HIPAA',['hipaa','protected health information','identifiable','face','charts','wristbands']],['Forms',['facility permission','subject permission','each photo','behind','copies','signatures']],['Privacy strategies',['mannequin','simulation','angle','shallow focus','clear the frame','crop']],['Ethics',['dignity','consent','delete','minors','guardian','copyright']]],model:'Because HIPAA protects identifiable health information, including full-face photographs of patients, I would make every photo HIPAA-compliant by focusing on the professional and avoiding identifiable patients. For each of my three photos, I would first ask the facility about its photography policy, then have an authorized representative sign the facility permission form and the professional—and any volunteer acting as a patient—sign the subject permission form, with a parent or guardian signing for minors. I would explain that the photos will appear in my printed portfolio, the online upload, display time and my presentation, that no names or health information will be included and that they may decline or ask me to delete a photo. To protect privacy, I would photograph a nurse practicing on a mannequin in a simulation lab, shoot a physical therapist over the shoulder with a consenting classmate whose health details are not shown and frame a pharmacist so no prescription labels are readable, using shallow focus to blur backgrounds and checking each frame for screens, charts, wristbands and whiteboards before shooting, since I can crop but cannot add graphics or blur effects later. I would bring extra blank forms, check signatures before leaving and file the forms behind each photo’s narrative—not in sheet protectors—placing copies of the facility form behind each narrative when two photos come from the same facility, and scanning them clearly for the PDF. I would also respect dignity and copyright and never post photos online without permission.'},sources:[RULES,HIPAA]};

export const HP4={title:'Camera basics: exposure, focus and color',hook:'Proper exposure and framing is worth 10 points per photo—30 points across the portfolio. Whether you use a phone or a digital camera, understanding how light, focus and color work lets you capture sharp, vibrant images in busy, sometimes dim health care settings. This lesson covers the technical foundations.',objectives:['Explain exposure and the exposure triangle.','Achieve sharp focus on the professional.','Manage white balance and color.','Choose camera settings for health care settings.'],sections:[
['What exposure means',[
'**Exposure** is the amount of light that reaches the camera’s sensor. **Underexposed** photos look dark and may lose detail in shadows; **overexposed** photos look washed out and lose detail in bright areas. The rubric’s top score describes a high-quality photo with proper exposure, a focused and framed subject, vibrant colors or effective black and white and effective lighting.',
'Phones adjust exposure automatically but let you **tap to set exposure** and slide brightness up or down. Many cameras offer exposure compensation and manual controls.',
'Check the image on screen after each shot—especially faces and hands.',
"Shooting in good light is easier than fixing exposure later. If the room is dim, ask whether more lights can be turned on or move to a brighter area before relying on editing.",
sort('Exposure problem?',['Underexposed','Overexposed','Well exposed'],[['Professional’s face in deep shadow',0],['White coat glowing with no detail',1],['Face and hands clear with natural tones',2],['Dark, grainy hallway image',0],['Window light washing out the scene',1]])]],
['The exposure triangle',[
'Three settings control exposure. **Aperture** (f-stop) is the lens opening: a wide aperture (small f-number like f/2) lets in more light and creates a **shallow depth of field** (blurred background). **Shutter speed** is how long the sensor is exposed: fast speeds (1/250 s) **freeze motion**, slow speeds (1/30 s) can cause blur. **ISO** is sensor sensitivity: higher ISO brightens images in low light but adds **grain (noise)**.',
'Balancing these three is the **exposure triangle**. In dim clinics, use a wide aperture and moderate ISO before slowing the shutter so movement stays sharp.',
"You do not need to master manual settings to succeed, but understanding the triangle helps you solve problems when automatic modes struggle, such as in dim rooms or with fast action.",
match('Setting and effect',[['Wide aperture (f/2)','More light and blurred background'],['Narrow aperture (f/11)','Less light and more in focus'],['Fast shutter (1/250 s)','Freezes motion'],['Slow shutter (1/15 s)','Motion blur risk'],['High ISO','Brighter but more grain'],['Low ISO','Cleaner image, needs more light']])]],
['Focus',[
'The professional—especially their **face, eyes or working hands**—should be in **sharp focus**. On a phone, **tap the subject** to focus and lock focus if available. On a camera, use single-point autofocus and place the point on the key area.',
'Hold the camera steady, brace your elbows or use a support, and take several shots of each moment. Zoom in on the screen afterward to check sharpness.',
'Portrait modes on phones can blur backgrounds, helping hide private details and emphasizing the professional.',
"If a professional moves quickly, use burst mode and select the sharpest frame. Movement is natural in health care tasks, and many shots increase your chance of a crisp image.",
chk('Where should focus usually be placed?',['On the professional’s eyes, face or working hands','On the background wall','On the floor','Anywhere'],'Clear center of interest.')]],
['White balance and color',[
'Different light sources have different **color temperatures**: indoor bulbs look warm (orange), fluorescent lights can look greenish and daylight or shade can look cool (blue). **White balance** corrects these casts so whites look white and skin tones look natural.',
'Use auto white balance, or set it for the light source. Avoid mixing very different lights in one scene. In editing, small color corrections are allowed; **black and white** can also be effective if it strengthens the image.',
"Shiny equipment and white coats can reflect light strongly. Watch for bright reflections on screens and metal surfaces, and change your angle slightly to reduce glare.",
ex('Fixing a color cast','A photo under clinic lights looks green (scenario invented for practice). Fix it.',['Identify the light: fluorescent overhead lights','Set white balance to fluorescent or use auto','Retake a test shot and check skin tones','If needed, adjust tint slightly in editing'],'Natural skin tones and clean whites (scenario invented for practice).')]],
['Phones versus cameras',[
'Modern **phones** produce excellent photos: use the main (1×) lens for best quality, avoid digital zoom (move closer instead), clean the lens and use HDR carefully in high-contrast scenes. **Digital cameras** offer more control over aperture, shutter and ISO and often perform better in low light.',
'Whatever you use, shoot at the **highest resolution** so 8″ × 10″ prints stay sharp, and keep the original files for editing.',
"If your phone supports RAW or high-efficiency formats, check that files can be edited and printed easily. JPEG at the highest quality is reliable for most printing services.",
sort('Good practice for print quality?',['Good','Poor'],[['Using the main 1× lens',0],['Heavy digital zoom',1],['Cleaning the lens before shooting',0],['Shooting at low resolution to save space',1],['Keeping original files',0]])]],
['Lighting in health care settings',[
'Many clinical spaces have harsh overhead fluorescent lights. Look for **window light**, position yourself so light falls on the professional’s face, and avoid having bright windows behind your subject. Flash can be harsh and distracting in clinical settings—ask before using it, and prefer natural or existing light.',
'Move a step or two to change the angle of light, or ask if a lamp can be turned on. Soft, even light flatters faces and shows equipment clearly.',
"Take a few test shots at the start of each session to check light and color. A minute of testing can prevent an entire set of underexposed or tinted photos.",
order('Improving light on site',['Observe where light comes from','Position the subject facing the light','Avoid bright windows behind the subject','Adjust exposure on screen','Take test shots and check faces','Ask before using flash'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Technical troubleshooting',{a:{text:'Your photo of a lab scientist is blurry because she moved while pipetting. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you change?',choices:[['Use a faster shutter speed and raise ISO slightly if needed','b','Correct.',true],['Use a slower shutter','b','Slower shutters increase motion blur.',false]]},b:{text:'The background shows a cluttered shelf. How can you reduce distraction?',choices:[['Use a wider aperture or portrait mode to blur the background, or change angle','c','Correct.',true],['Add a fake background in editing','c','Adding backgrounds is not allowed.',false]]},c:{text:'Your photo is sharp, well exposed and focused on the professional.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Exposure is the light reaching the sensor; underexposed images are dark, overexposed images are washed out; tap to set exposure and check faces and hands (exposure and framing is 10 points per photo).','The exposure triangle: aperture (wide = more light and blurred background), shutter speed (fast freezes motion) and ISO (higher = brighter but grainier); focus sharply on the professional’s face or working hands.','Correct white balance for warm, fluorescent or daylight sources; use the main lens, avoid digital zoom and shoot at high resolution for 8″ × 10″ prints; use soft natural light facing the subject and ask before using flash.'],terms:[['Exposure','The amount of light reaching the camera sensor.'],['Aperture','The lens opening that controls light and depth of field.'],['White balance','A setting that corrects color casts from different light sources.']],mcqs:[q('What does a fast shutter speed do?','Freezes motion','Blurs motion','Adds grain','Changes color','Shutter.'),q('A wide aperture (small f-number) creates:','A blurred background','More in focus','Less light','Grain','Depth of field.'),q('Raising ISO:','Brightens images but adds grain','Darkens images','Freezes motion','Fixes color casts','ISO.'),q('Where should focus usually be?','On the professional’s face or working hands','On the background','On the ceiling','On a random object','Focus.'),q('Fluorescent lights often cause:','A greenish color cast','Perfect color','Motion blur','Grain','White balance.'),q('Why avoid digital zoom?','It reduces image quality','It improves sharpness','It is required','It changes white balance','Quality.'),q('How many points is exposure and framing worth per photo?','10','5','15','20','Rubric.'),q('Where should bright windows be?','Not behind the subject','Directly behind the subject','Covered with graphics','In every photo','Lighting.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would use exposure, focus and color to take high-quality photos in a health care setting.',rubric:[['Exposure',['exposure','underexposed','overexposed','tap','check']],['Triangle',['aperture','shutter','iso','motion','grain','depth of field']],['Focus and color',['focus','face','hands','white balance','color cast','black and white']],['Practice',['main lens','digital zoom','resolution','window light','flash','test shots']]],model:'Exposure is the amount of light reaching the camera’s sensor, and each photo’s exposure and framing is worth 10 points, so I would check every shot for faces and hands that are neither too dark nor washed out. On my phone, I would tap the professional to set focus and exposure, sliding brightness as needed. I would balance the exposure triangle: in a dim clinic, I would use a wide aperture or portrait mode to let in more light and blur distracting backgrounds, keep the shutter speed fast enough—around 1/250 second—to freeze a lab scientist’s pipetting motion and raise ISO only as much as needed to avoid excessive grain. I would focus on the professional’s eyes or working hands, brace my elbows to stay steady, take several shots of each moment and zoom in afterward to check sharpness. Because fluorescent lights can cast a green tint and indoor bulbs look orange, I would use auto white balance or set it for the light source, avoid mixing light types and make only small color corrections later, or choose black and white if it strengthened the image. I would use my phone’s main 1× lens, avoid digital zoom by moving closer, clean the lens and shoot at the highest resolution for sharp 8-by-10-inch prints. For lighting, I would position the professional facing window light, avoid bright windows behind them, take test shots and ask before using flash, which can be harsh and distracting in clinical spaces.'},sources:[RULES,PURDUE]};

export const HP5={title:'Composition and storytelling',hook:'A technically perfect photo can still fail if viewers cannot tell what career it shows or why it matters. Two rubric items per photo—focus of occupation and originality—reward images with a clear center of interest that bring the occupation to life and make viewers feel something. Composition is how you tell the story of a career in one frame.',objectives:['Create a clear center of interest on the professional at work.','Apply composition techniques such as the rule of thirds and leading lines.','Capture authentic action and emotion.','Make each photo creative and memorable.'],sections:[
['Focus of occupation',[
'The top score for **focus of occupation** goes to a photo with a **clear center of interest**: a clearly defined health professional, visible and identifiable, performing an aspect of their job, so the career is **easily defined**. Lower scores describe photos where the job task is not visible, the career is unclear or the photo does not focus on the person performing the occupation.',
'Ask yourself: if someone saw this photo without a caption, could they name the career? Include **career clues**—equipment, uniforms, setting and the task itself.',
"Including the professional’s face along with the task usually strengthens focus of occupation, because expressions communicate concentration and care that equipment alone cannot show.",
sort('Career easily identified?',['Easy to identify','Hard to identify'],[['A pharmacist counting tablets behind a labeled counter',0],['A person in scrubs standing in a hallway',1],['A sonographer holding an ultrasound probe beside the machine',0],['A close-up of gloved hands only',1],['A physical therapist guiding a patient on parallel bars',0]])]],
['Composition techniques',[
'**Rule of thirds:** place the professional’s face or the key action along grid lines or intersections. **Leading lines:** use counters, hallways or equipment lines to guide the eye to the subject. **Framing:** use doorways or equipment to frame the professional. **Fill the frame:** move closer to remove distractions. **Depth:** include a foreground element, such as equipment, to add dimension.',
'Keep the **background clean** and check edges for distractions before you shoot.',
"Look for natural frames and lines before the professional begins working. Planning composition ahead lets you focus on timing once the action starts.",
match('Technique and example',[['Rule of thirds','Therapist’s face on the upper-left intersection'],['Leading lines','Pharmacy shelves leading to the pharmacist'],['Framing','Doorway framing a nurse at a workstation'],['Fill the frame','Moving closer to a lab scientist at a microscope'],['Foreground depth','Ultrasound screen in the foreground, sonographer behind'],['Clean background','Removing clutter from the counter']])]],
['Angles and perspective',[
'**Eye-level** shots feel natural and respectful. **Low angles** can make a professional look confident and capable. **Over-the-shoulder** shots show what the professional is focused on. **Close-ups** of hands at work show skill; **environmental portraits** show the professional within their workplace.',
'Try several angles for each moment and choose the strongest. Moving a few steps often transforms an ordinary photo.',
"Be careful that unusual angles still show the career clearly. A creative angle that hides the task can lower the focus of occupation score.",
"Get down to the level of the task when possible. Photographing a therapist and patient at eye level with their exercise, rather than from standing height, often creates a more engaging image.",
chk('Which angle best shows what a lab scientist is examining?',['Over-the-shoulder toward the microscope and sample','Directly at the ceiling','From across the building','Behind a closed door'],'Show the task.')]],
['Capturing authentic action',[
'The best photos show **real tasks** in progress rather than stiff poses. Ask the professional to perform a typical task while you photograph continuously. Use **burst mode** to catch the decisive moment—when hands, eyes and equipment align.',
'Encourage natural expressions: talk with the professional while shooting, and photograph moments of concentration, communication or reassurance. Authentic emotion makes viewers feel connected.',
'If you simulate a task, make it accurate—ask the professional to demonstrate correctly.',
"Explain to the professional that you are looking for natural moments, not poses. Many people relax once they realize they can simply work while you photograph.",
order('Capturing the decisive moment',['Ask the professional to perform a typical task','Choose your angle and frame','Set focus and exposure','Shoot in bursts as the action unfolds','Review and reshoot if needed','Select the strongest moment'])]],
['Originality and emotion',[
'The top **originality** score describes a photo that is **creative and unique**, **memorable** and lets the viewer **feel the emotion** of the image—bringing the occupation “to life.” Look beyond the obvious shot: an unusual angle, dramatic but natural light, a reflection, a moment of connection between professional and patient (with consent) or a striking detail.',
'Study health care photography in magazines and professional association websites for inspiration, but create your own original images.',
"Ask a few people who do not know your project to look at each photo and describe the career and mood. Their reactions show whether the image communicates what you intended.",
"Emotion does not have to be dramatic. A small smile of encouragement or a moment of careful concentration can make a photo feel human and memorable.",
ex('Making a photo more original','Improve an ordinary photo (scenario invented for practice).',['Ordinary: a respiratory therapist standing next to a ventilator, facing the camera','Improvement 1: photograph during a real setup task','Improvement 2: low angle with the ventilator display framing the therapist','Improvement 3: capture the moment the therapist checks settings with focused expression'],'An active, framed, emotionally engaging image (scenario invented for practice).')]],
['Telling a story in each photo',[
'Each photo should tell a mini-story: **who** (the professional), **what** (the task), **where** (the setting) and **why it matters** (care, safety, healing). Think about what you want viewers to understand about the career and compose to show it.',
'Your Round Two presentation will describe the story behind each photo, so remember details of what was happening and why.',
"Choose photos that work together as a set while each telling a unique story. Consistent quality and varied subjects make the portfolio feel polished.",
"Before finalizing, ask yourself whether each photo would make a viewer curious about the career. Curiosity is a sign of a strong, memorable image.",
sort('Supports the photo’s story?',['Supports the story','Distracts'],[['The professional’s focused expression',0],['A coworker’s lunch on the counter',1],['Equipment used in the task',0],['A random poster in the background',1],['The patient-care setting',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Composition choices',{a:{text:'Your photo shows a nurse far away in a large room. (Scenario invented for practice.) What would improve focus of occupation?',choices:[['Move closer so the nurse and task fill the frame','b','Correct.',true],['Add a graphic arrow pointing to the nurse','b','Graphics are not allowed; compose better instead.',false]]},b:{text:'All your shots are posed with professionals smiling at the camera. How can you add originality?',choices:[['Capture them performing real tasks with natural expressions','c','Correct.',true],['Keep the posed shots','c','Action and emotion score higher.',false]]},c:{text:'Your photos now show careers in action and feel alive.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Focus of occupation (10 per photo) needs a clear center of interest—an identifiable professional performing a job task so the career is easily defined; include career clues.','Use rule of thirds, leading lines, framing, filling the frame, foreground depth and clean backgrounds; try eye-level, low, over-the-shoulder, close-up and environmental angles.','Capture authentic action with burst mode and natural expressions; originality (10 per photo) rewards creative, memorable images that bring the occupation to life; tell a who-what-where-why story in each frame.'],terms:[['Center of interest','The main subject that draws the viewer’s eye.'],['Leading lines','Lines in a scene that guide the eye toward the subject.'],['Decisive moment','The instant when action and composition come together.']],mcqs:[q('What does focus of occupation require?','An identifiable professional performing a job task','Only equipment','Only a building','A selfie','Rubric.'),q('The rule of thirds places the subject:','Along grid lines or intersections','Always dead center','Off the frame','Upside down','Composition.'),q('Leading lines:','Guide the eye toward the subject','Distract viewers','Add graphics','Blur the background','Technique.'),q('Which angle shows what a professional is examining?','Over the shoulder','From the ceiling only','From outside the building','Behind a door','Perspective.'),q('Burst mode helps:','Capture the decisive moment','Add backgrounds','Fix HIPAA problems','Print photos','Action.'),q('Originality rewards photos that:','Are creative and evoke emotion','Are posed and stiff','Include the competitor','Use collages','Rubric.'),q('Which item distracts from a photo’s story?','A coworker’s lunch on the counter','The task’s equipment','The professional’s expression','The care setting','Clutter.'),q('Is adding a graphic arrow allowed?','No','Yes','Only in black and white','Only for photo #3','Editing rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would compose a health career photo that scores well for focus of occupation and originality.',rubric:[['Center of interest',['clear','identifiable','task','career','clues','center of interest']],['Composition',['rule of thirds','leading lines','framing','fill the frame','background']],['Angles and action',['angle','over-the-shoulder','low','burst','authentic','natural']],['Originality and story',['creative','emotion','memorable','story','who','why']]],model:'For my photo of a respiratory therapist, I would make the therapist the clear center of interest, performing a real task—setting up a nebulizer or checking a ventilator in a simulation lab—so anyone could identify the career from equipment, uniform and setting. I would move close enough to fill the frame and clear distracting items from the counter, then place the therapist’s focused face on an upper rule-of-thirds intersection, using the lines of the ventilator tubing to lead the eye toward their hands. I would try several angles: a low angle to show confidence, an over-the-shoulder view toward the ventilator display and an environmental portrait showing the respiratory care setting, choosing the strongest. Rather than a stiff pose, I would ask the therapist to perform the setup as usual while I talked with them and shot in burst mode to capture the decisive moment when their eyes, hands and the equipment aligned. For originality, I might frame the therapist through the ventilator’s display in the foreground or use soft window light to create an emotional, memorable image that brings the occupation to life. Each photo would tell a story—who the professional is, what task they are doing, where they work and why it matters for patients’ breathing—and I would remember these details for Round Two.'},sources:[RULES,PURDUE]};
